---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MonteCarloRetirement from '@/components/tools/finance/MonteCarloRetirement.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="monte-carlo-retirement-calculator">
  <MonteCarloRetirement client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to read the result</h2>
    <p>
      The large percentage is the share of simulated retirements in which the money lasts to the age you
      plan for. All amounts are in today's money, so inflation is already removed. The chart shows the
      spread of outcomes, and the table shows how the chance changes with the retirement age and the
      monthly spending.
    </p>

    <h2>How the simulation works</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        It takes each month's real return from US stock market data since 1871, with dividends reinvested.
      </li>
      <li>
        It builds a life from random 5-year runs of consecutive months. Runs keep crashes and recoveries
        together, as they happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each month the balance grows by the stock share at that month's return and by the rest at the fixed
        return you set. Then it takes your saving or your spending.
      </li>
      <li>A path fails in the month its balance reaches zero.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why one average return is not enough</h2>
    <p>
      Two retirees with the same average return can end very differently. If the bad years come first, the
      retiree sells at low prices, and the money that is gone cannot recover. This is sequence-of-returns
      risk. The plain <a href="/retirement-calculator">retirement calculator</a> uses one steady return, so
      it cannot show this risk.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it runs fast</h2>
    <p>
      The table needs 81 simulations. Your processor runs them in parallel in compiled WebAssembly, so the
      result changes as you type and nothing leaves the tab.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
